\b MyFps UE5.5 Technical Summary - Data-Driven Weapon System Refactor\b0\par
\par
1. Goal\par
\par
The goal of this refactor was to move the project away from the old UTP_WeaponComponent-centered weapon actor model and toward a data-driven multiplayer FPS weapon architecture. The new default flow is based on WeaponDefinition assets, with character-owned Inventory, View, and Combat components handling state, presentation, and combat behavior separately.\par
\par
The intent was not to delete every legacy path immediately. The safer milestone was to make the default game flow fully use the new system while keeping UTP_WeaponComponent as a compatibility path for older map actors and test assets.\par
\par
2. Problems in the Previous Design\par
\par
The old system concentrated pickup, ownership, firing, reload, mesh attachment, dropped-weapon physics, highlight, prompt UI, ammo replication, and cosmetics inside UTP_WeaponComponent. It worked, but it was hard to scale.\par
\par
Main issues:\par
- Weapon data and weapon behavior were tightly coupled.\par
- First-person and third-person weapon presentation were tied to the same real weapon actor, which caused visibility and attachment problems in multiplayer.\par
- The default GameMode still spawned the old BP_Weapon, so new and legacy systems could both participate in the same playtest.\par
- Fully physical dropped weapons could collide with players and cause launch/flying bugs, while fully non-physical weapons floated in the air.\par
- Ammo, reload, tracer, damage, pickup, and attachment all depended on clean replicated ownership state.\par
\par
3. New Architecture\par
\par
The new weapon system is split into four main layers.\par
\par
UMyFpsWeaponDefinition is the static weapon data asset. It stores weapon ID, display name, ammo settings, damage, fire interval, reload time, first-person and third-person meshes, attachment sockets, muzzle settings, montages, sounds, and tracer VFX. Adding a new weapon should primarily mean creating a new data asset rather than duplicating code.\par
\par
UMyFpsWeaponInventoryComponent is the replicated weapon state component on the character. It stores the current weapon definition and runtime state such as current clip ammo, reserve ammo, reloading state, firing state, fire sequence, and whether the character is using a legacy weapon actor.\par
\par
UMyFpsWeaponViewComponent owns local weapon presentation. It creates and attaches first-person and third-person weapon meshes. The first-person mesh attaches to Mesh1P using WeaponPoint, and the third-person mesh attaches to the character mesh using Weapon_R. Muzzle location is resolved from the visible weapon mesh through MuzzlePoint.\par
\par
UMyFpsWeaponCombatComponent owns firing and reload behavior. It handles input requests, server validation, ammo consumption, server-side BulletTrace damage, reload timers, multicast cosmetics, and Niagara tracer spawning.\par
\par
4. Network Authority Model\par
\par
The rule is: final gameplay state is changed only on the server. Clients request actions and play presentation.\par
\par
Fire flow:\par
Client presses fire -> ServerStartFire -> server validates match state, death state, weapon availability, ammo, and reload state -> consumes ammo -> performs BulletTrace on the server -> applies damage -> multicasts fire cosmetics -> each client resolves its local muzzle and spawns the tracer.\par
\par
Reload flow:\par
Client presses R -> ServerReload -> server validates reload eligibility -> sets bReloading -> multicasts reload cosmetics -> server waits ReloadTime -> FinishReload updates ammo -> Inventory replication refreshes client UI.\par
\par
Pickup flow:\par
The overlap prompt is local-only. Pressing E calls ServerInteractPickup. The server validates distance and pickup state, equips the WeaponDefinition into the character inventory, disables the pickup, and destroys it.\par
\par
Drop flow:\par
Client presses G -> ServerDropCurrentWeapon -> server reads the current WeaponDefinition -> spawns AMyFpsWeaponPickupActor -> writes the WeaponDefinition into the pickup -> clears Inventory -> the pickup replicates to all clients.\par
\par
5. Pickup and Dropped Weapon Handling\par
\par
AMyFpsWeaponPickupActor is now only the world pickup representation. It no longer owns the full combat behavior of the equipped weapon. It contains PickupMesh, InteractionSphere, and PickupPromptWidget.\par
\par
PickupMesh is used for world display, crosshair trace, and custom-depth highlight. InteractionSphere reuses TP_PickUpComponent for local overlap prompts and interaction routing. The prompt text changes between pickup and replace based on whether the player already has a weapon.\par
\par
When dropped, the pickup briefly enables SimulatePhysics and Gravity, receives a small forward/up impulse, and ignores Pawn collision. After DroppedPhysicsDuration, physics is disabled and the pickup returns to QueryOnly interaction mode. This gives a visible drop-to-ground motion without keeping unstable physics active long term.\par
\par
6. Default Flow Migration\par
\par
GameMode no longer searches for or spawns /Game/BluePrint/Weapon/BP_Weapon as a fallback starting weapon. Starting weapons for match start, respawn, and next round now only use StartingWeaponDefinition. If BP_GameMode does not configure StartingWeaponDefinition, the system logs a warning and gives no weapon instead of silently using the legacy actor.\par
\par
This is important because it makes the default combat flow unambiguous: the player weapon comes from the new data-driven inventory path.\par
\par
7. Required UE Setup\par
\par
BP_GameMode:\par
- Set Weapon / StartingWeaponDefinition to DA_Rifle.\par
\par
BP_FirstPersonCharacter:\par
- Set Weapon / Drop / WeaponPickupActorClass to the new pickup blueprint, such as BP_WeaponPickup_Rifle_New or a generic pickup blueprint.\par
- Tune DroppedWeaponForwardImpulse, DroppedWeaponUpImpulse, and DroppedPhysicsDuration for game feel.\par
\par
DA_Rifle:\par
- Set FirstPersonWeaponMesh and ThirdPersonWeaponMesh.\par
- First-person attach socket: WeaponPoint.\par
- Third-person attach socket: Weapon_R.\par
- Muzzle socket or component: MuzzlePoint.\par
- Set Fire, Reload, Equip montages, sounds, TracerEffect, FireMontageStopDelay, and ReloadMontageStopDelay.\par
\par
Weapon Skeletal Mesh:\par
- A Physics Asset is required for dropped-weapon physics.\par
- Use simple collision bodies, low restitution, and high friction to avoid bouncing and excessive spinning.\par
\par
8. Current Completion State\par
\par
Completed:\par
- WeaponDefinition data asset framework.\par
- AssetManager registration.\par
- Replicated Inventory state.\par
- New PickupActor, crosshair highlight, and local pickup prompt.\par
- ViewComponent for first-person and third-person weapon meshes.\par
- CombatComponent for server firing, damage, reload, and tracer.\par
- New-system drop, replacement, and death drop.\par
- Short dropped-weapon physics followed by a frozen QueryOnly pickup state.\par
- GameMode starting weapon migration to StartingWeaponDefinition.\par
\par
Kept for compatibility:\par
- UTP_WeaponComponent still exists.\par
- Character input still has legacy branches so old weapon actors in maps can be tested.\par
- The legacy path is no longer used by default starting weapons.\par
\par
9. Suggested Next Work\par
\par
The next phase should focus on portfolio-level polish rather than expanding the weapon foundation immediately:\par
- Hit marker, kill feedback, and kill sound.\par
- Weapon slots and weapon switching.\par
- Ammo and reload UI polish.\par
- Better client-side feel under latency, including local fire prediction and smoother tracer presentation.\par
- Final retirement of UTP_WeaponComponent after all old map actors have been replaced.
}
